WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.860
It's one thing to be lauded as being at the
top of your profession, but it's a different

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:08.410
sort of mind that belongs to someone who just
quits for a while, because they think they've

00:00:08.410 --> 00:00:14.070
already solved all the problems and answered
all the discussions in their field.

00:00:14.070 --> 00:00:19.900
That's exactly what Ludwig Wittgenstein did
after he published his Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
in 1922.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.480
It would be the only book he'd publish in
his lifetime, and with it, he was convinced

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.140
that he'd finished the discussion about philosophy.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:32.580
So, he did what anyone would do after they've
finished: they quit.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:36.480
His hiatus wouldn't last long, but it's a
fascinating glimpse into the man who would

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:40.350
be remembered as one of the 20th century's
greatest philosophers.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:45.670
He was a self-loathing genius, a man born
into wealth and the aristocracy, a man who

00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:50.149
knew all too well what it was like to have
everything taken away in the blink of an eye.

00:00:50.149 --> 00:00:55.250
He gave away a huge amount of money, fought
and lived through World Wars, saw the darkest

00:00:55.250 --> 00:00:59.359
side of human nature — and through it all,
he struggled to make sense of it.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:03.960
He may not have solved all the problems of
philosophy, but he definitely gave the world

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.190
something to think about.

00:01:06.190 --> 00:01:17.400
Did he create Hitler and the Third Reich?

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.409
Ludwig Wittgenstein was the youngest of a
very wealthy family, but that's not to say

00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:24.560
that he belonged to old money.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:28.549
His father, Karl, was the son of a wool merchant,
and spent his early life much like the rest

00:01:28.549 --> 00:01:33.610
of us — rebelling against his own father's
wishes and working some strange, odd jobs

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.930
before finally falling into where he needed
to be.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:40.060
At one point, he even headed to the US with
nothing but a violin, hoping to make a living

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:41.299
as an entertainer.

00:01:41.299 --> 00:01:46.320
Unfortunately for the young Karl, the assassination
of President Abraham Lincoln put a hold on

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:50.500
all forms of public entertainment, and he
was forced to find work as a barkeeper and

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:51.500
a teacher.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.760
He eventually returned to his home in Vienna,
was reconciled with his parents, and was hired

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:59.259
to work as a draftsman on the construction
of a steel mill.

00:01:59.259 --> 00:02:04.680
His elders had their doubts, but he was "extraordinarily
able and industrious," and he impressed them

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:09.280
even further when he developed a knack for
sourcing steel and iron during a period of

00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:10.800
economic crisis.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:15.230
It wasn't long before he was making investments,
profiting from the growth of the rails, and

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:19.350
finally making a fortune off the 1878 war
between Russia and Turkey.

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:24.290
By the time Ludwig Wittgenstein was born in
1889, his family had money and influence.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:28.060
They moved through all the social circles
of the upper echelons of Vienna's cultural

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:32.810
and intellectual crowds, and their wealth
only continued to grow at a rate that allowed

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:36.550
Karl to retire in 1898, at the age of 51.

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:40.650
Theirs was a life of leisure; they amassed
collections of art and manuscripts, furniture

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:41.970
and porcelain.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:44.980
And Karl's siblings — all 11 of them — were
just as successful.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:50.260
It was a family of scientists and soldiers,
of great thinkers, of judges, of patrons of

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.980
the arts, and doctors.

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:57.420
The Wittgenstein home was a hustle and bustle
of Viennese culture, and Karl's children grew

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:02.830
up in a home frequented by the likes of Sigmund
Freud, Johannes Brahms, and Karl Kraus.

00:03:02.830 --> 00:03:07.460
It's also during this time that Wittgenstein
read one of the books that would have a lifelong

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:11.950
impact on him: Otto Weininger's 1903 book
Sex and Character.

00:03:11.950 --> 00:03:16.310
While it's still debated just how much of
an impact the book had, it is generally credited

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:22.060
for introducing Wittgenstein to the idea that
life should be lived in an almost single-minded

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:25.570
pursuit of the creation of a work of pure
genius.

00:03:25.570 --> 00:03:30.200
Lofty aspirations, for sure, but hearing some
of the names he grew up around, it's easy

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:35.900
to see why he would be so determined to achieve
greatness and immortality through his work.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:40.750
But that good fortune that shaped Karl's generation
did not extend to the next.

00:03:40.750 --> 00:03:45.520
Our Wittgenstein would leave the family home
for Cambridge in 1911, and by then, he had

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:48.060
already lived through the deaths of several
siblings.

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:53.040
His oldest brother, Hans, headed off to America
and disappeared in 1902 — the family believed

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.370
he took his own life.

00:03:54.370 --> 00:03:59.820
In 1904, the second oldest, Rudy, did the
same by drinking a glass of potassium cyanide.

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:03.430
As a way of coping, Karl forbid the rest of
the family from ever mentioning either of

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:08.120
their names again, and it was an order that
didn't just push an already devastated family

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:10.850
to the breaking point, it broke them.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.470
The relationship between the remaining children
and their parents was never the same, and

00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.380
it explained quite a bit about what would
happen later.

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.540
Even as a young Ludwig Wittgenstein suffered
through the loss of his brothers, it's worth

00:04:21.540 --> 00:04:26.250
taking a slight aside here to address one
of those rumors that sounds so unlikely, so

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:28.720
strange, that it has to be false.

00:04:28.720 --> 00:04:31.750
And in this case… it's only partially false.

00:04:31.750 --> 00:04:35.560
Historians and scholars have long tried to
figure out just what drove Hitler's anti-Semetic

00:04:35.560 --> 00:04:40.880
madness, and there's a single mention in Mein
Kampf that goes like this: "I did meet one

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:46.280
Jewish boy who was treated by all of us with
caution, but only because various experiences

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:50.910
had led us to doubt his discretion and we
did not particularly trust him…"

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:56.680
That's led to a lot of speculation that Wittgenstein
was that boy, as he and Adolf Hitler did — albeit

00:04:56.680 --> 00:04:59.430
briefly — attend the same school in Linz.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:03.630
It was the 1904-1905 school year, and it's
a rewarding theory.

00:05:03.630 --> 00:05:07.590
Not only would both go on to be written about
in the history books, but it would help make

00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:12.200
sense of one of the biggest questions of the
Holocaust: Why?

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:16.970
Wittenstein and his family would feel the
icy grip of the hand of the Third Reich, but

00:05:16.970 --> 00:05:19.450
that's for a bit later in the tale.

00:05:19.450 --> 00:05:24.870
The first strike against this story that Wittgenstein
inspired Hitler's rabid hatred of the Jews

00:05:24.870 --> 00:05:29.880
is that for three generations, the Wittgensteins
had been practicing Roman Catholics, and he

00:05:29.880 --> 00:05:34.790
didn't necessarily identify as Jewish — even
though baptismal records identified three

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:38.120
of his four grandparents as being Jewish before
their conversion.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:40.630
And that, too, is going to become important.

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:44.500
And there's an end to that quote that often
gets omitted from the conspiracy theories:

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.080
"... but neither I nor the others had any
thoughts on the matter."

00:05:48.080 --> 00:05:51.980
It's generally believed that it wasn't until
much later that Hitler's anti-Semetism kicked

00:05:51.980 --> 00:05:56.440
into high gear, and it's worth noting that
mainstream history has pretty much cleared

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:59.810
Wittgenstein of being the root cause of Hitler's
anti-Semetism.

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:03.120
"I shall certainly encourage him.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.090
Perhaps he will do great things…"

00:06:07.090 --> 00:06:10.860
Wittgenstein's education was as erratic as
his father's young life had been.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:15.400
He didn't start in philosophy, in fact, he
started out about as far from philosophy as

00:06:15.400 --> 00:06:16.400
possible.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.400
After studying mechanical engineering in Berlin,
he switched to aeronautics — and particularly

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:22.700
the study of kites — in 1908.

00:06:22.700 --> 00:06:26.270
That's also when he headed off to Manchester
in England, and his work there led him down

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:27.730
the road to mathematics.

00:06:27.730 --> 00:06:31.889
Finding himself pondering heavy philosophical
musings over the foundations of mathematics

00:06:31.889 --> 00:06:35.520
as a whole, he sought out the mathematician
and philosopher Gottlob Frege.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:40.010
It was Frege who recommended that he speak
with Bertrand Russell, and it was Russell

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:44.140
who saw the potential for "great things" in
the young student.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:48.280
Wittgenstein saw the potential for great things
in himself, too, once asking Russell, "will

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.930
you please tell me whether I am a complete
idiot or not?"

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:56.460
When Russell asked him why, he responded with,
"Because, if I am a complete idiot, I shall

00:06:56.460 --> 00:07:00.730
become an aeronaut; but, if not, I shall become
a philosopher."

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:04.070
Russell instructed him to write a bit of philosophical
argument, and he did.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:08.800
Russell read just a single sentence and told
him, "No, you must not become an aeronaut."

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:13.470
Wittgenstein only spent a few years at Cambridge,
and a year after meeting Russell, he had spent

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:17.680
so much time and effort in the pursuit of
logic that Russell declared there was nothing

00:07:17.680 --> 00:07:18.680
more to teach him.

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:22.790
He said, too, that he was difficult — he
would often show up in his mentor's rooms

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:26.720
in the middle of the night, pace relentlessly,
and declare that when he left, he was going

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.860
to kill himself.

00:07:27.860 --> 00:07:31.280
Russell wouldn't send him away, and they would
sit in silence for hours.

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:36.090
Finally, Russell asked him if he was pondering
the mysteries of logic or of his sins, and

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:37.650
responded simply, "Both."

00:07:37.650 --> 00:07:41.611
And he took his deep thoughts very seriously,
heading off to Norway for months at a time

00:07:41.611 --> 00:07:44.830
to simply think without the distractions of
campus life.

00:07:44.830 --> 00:07:48.640
He built himself a wooden hut at the edge
of a fjord, and it was there that he laid

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.660
the groundwork for his beliefs that facts
can be shared through what he called "logical

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:54.660
forms".

00:07:54.660 --> 00:07:59.300
Wittgenstein left Cambridge in 1913 and it
was a year marked by the passing of his father.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.800
He quickly gave away his inheritance and by
this time, it was the eve of World War One.

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:07.280
The following year he voluntarily enlisted
in the Austrian Army, and far from being scared,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.460
his writing suggests he was looking forward
to the war and to the opportunity of getting

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:13.330
up close and personal with death.

00:08:13.330 --> 00:08:18.020
He wrote in one journal "only death gives
life its meaning," and at first, he did spend

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.290
quite a bit of the war well out of harm's
way.

00:08:20.290 --> 00:08:24.050
When it came time for him to volunteer for
a posting, though, he chose one of the most

00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:28.380
dangerous: he headed out into no man's land,
and manned a lookout post at the front of

00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:29.470
the Austrian line.

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:33.710
Throughout the war, Wittgenstein continued
to write both philosophy and to those he had

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.370
met and studied with at Cambridge.

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.010
His admiration of Russell — and Russell's
appreciation of his intellect — perhaps

00:08:39.010 --> 00:08:40.919
predictably began to go downhill.

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:45.450
He was as difficult as he was brilliant, their
relationship turned into one of bickering,

00:08:45.450 --> 00:08:49.220
and a regular theme in Wittgenstein's journals
was one of lament.

00:08:49.220 --> 00:08:52.381
He was heartbroken at the responses he was
getting from his fellow philosophers: even

00:08:52.381 --> 00:08:56.390
if he survived the war, he wrote, he wondered
about the usefulness of his work and worried

00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:00.140
that no one would understand it — or be
able to grasp how important it was.

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:03.580
He wrote constantly during the war, compiling
notes for what would eventually be turned

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:09.760
into his Tractatus Logico-Philosopicus — or,
in translation, his Logical-Philosophical

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:10.760
Treatise.

00:09:10.760 --> 00:09:14.950
It would be the only book he would publish
in his lifetime, and scholars that look back

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:19.800
over the work in context of what he was going
through at the time say it was undoubtedly

00:09:19.800 --> 00:09:24.480
influenced by the war, and shaped by both
his time in relative safety behind the front

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:26.900
lines, and in the thick of the fighting.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.529
It was at this time, too, that he read Tolstoy's
The Gospel in Brief, and had something of

00:09:31.529 --> 00:09:32.529
an epiphany.

00:09:32.529 --> 00:09:36.730
He read it again and again as wrote his own
works on logic, work that was eventually led

00:09:36.730 --> 00:09:40.880
in the direction of combining ethics, religion,
and aesthetics with logic.

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:45.900
At the heart of the matter was that all of
those topics couldn't be satisfactorily explained

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:48.570
with words, they needed to be shown.

00:09:48.570 --> 00:09:49.570
Confusing?

00:09:49.570 --> 00:09:53.589
Absolutely, and he said so himself when he
wrote a friend with this insight: "If only

00:09:53.589 --> 00:09:57.490
you do not try to utter what is unutterable,
then nothing gets lost.

00:09:57.490 --> 00:10:02.490
But the unutterable will be — unutterably
— contained in what has been uttered."

00:10:02.490 --> 00:10:07.080
And that sums up how difficult Wittgenstein
can be to read, much less understand.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:12.880
In 1918, he was captured, and spent the remainder
of the war in an Italian POW camp; here, too,

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.760
he kept writing, and when he was released,
he published his seminal work.

00:10:16.760 --> 00:10:21.350
Summing it up briefly is just impossible,
but it's worth nothing that not only did Bertrand

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:26.290
Russell write the original introduction for
it, but Wittgenstein was fairly disgusted

00:10:26.290 --> 00:10:29.200
with what he thought was a complete misunderstanding
of the text.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.250
At the heart of it is the idea that the world
consists of objects.

00:10:32.250 --> 00:10:37.450
In turn, combinations of objects result in
what he described as a state of affairs, and

00:10:37.450 --> 00:10:42.690
facts were ultimately states of affairs that
existed in the real world, and could be described

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:44.270
via those objects.

00:10:44.270 --> 00:10:45.290
Basically.

00:10:45.290 --> 00:10:48.560
Wittgenstein had a reason for exploring all
of this, and he was trying to construct a

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:53.910
system that would allow for a framework with
which to divide the world into two areas:

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.750
"sense and nonsense."

00:10:55.750 --> 00:10:59.680
Interestingly, some of the things he defined
as "senseless" under his system were things

00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:03.600
like mathematics and logic, while nonsense
was something else completely.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.250
That was the idea that something is completely
devoid of meaning, and well, he had a lot

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:09.240
of time on his hands during the war.

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:12.710
Considering that even the professionals in
the world of philosophy have trouble describing

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:17.589
his work and tracing the leaps and bounds
he makes in his logic, we're just going to

00:11:17.589 --> 00:11:20.480
leave this one right here.

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:23.190
And that's exactly what Wittgenstein did,
too.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:27.160
After the publication of his work, he was
completely convinced that there were simply

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:29.220
no more problems left to solve.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:34.740
He quit philosophy and took on a less complicated
life, spending nearly a decade traveling around

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:39.850
Vienna, taking on odd jobs from gardening
to architect, and occasionally teaching.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:44.550
That particular vocation came to an abrupt
and rather brutal end; in one Austrian mountain

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:48.170
village, he struck a student so hard he knocked
the boy unconscious.

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:52.529
His immediate resignation followed, along
with a long bout of depression.

00:11:52.529 --> 00:11:55.899
Fortunately for the world of philosophy, he
was ultimately exposed to the philosophical

00:11:55.899 --> 00:12:01.690
musings of the Vienna Circle, a group of mathematicians,
scientists, and philosophers who met regularly

00:12:01.690 --> 00:12:02.690
through the 1920s.

00:12:02.690 --> 00:12:13.589
Thanks to them, he realized that there were
still quite a few problems worthy of his attention.

00:12:13.589 --> 00:12:14.589
The dawn of World War Two

00:12:14.589 --> 00:12:18.060
The realization that there were bigger, better,
and deeper philosophical problems that remained

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:23.850
unsolved led to Wittgentein's return to Cambridge
in 1929; at the time, he wasn't the most famous

00:12:23.850 --> 00:12:24.850
member of his family.

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:29.190
That honor went to his brother, Paul, who
had always had an innate gift for music, and

00:12:29.190 --> 00:12:31.390
was known as an outstanding concert pianist.

00:12:31.390 --> 00:12:35.589
He, too, was in World War One, and suffered
an injury that should have been devastating:

00:12:35.589 --> 00:12:36.600
he lost an arm.

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:41.180
But even as his brother wandered Vienna looking
for a point to his continued existence on

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:45.180
Earth, Paul worked with some of the era's
greatest composers to create music for the

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:48.250
piano that required only a left hand to play.

00:12:48.250 --> 00:12:52.021
As the world slowly inched toward yet another
conflict that would engulf the globe, the

00:12:52.021 --> 00:12:57.290
Austrian brothers found themselves in perhaps
the best of places they could be: on the outside.

00:12:57.290 --> 00:13:02.050
In 1939, Wittgenstein was elected to one of
the top positions in Cambridge professorship,

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:05.550
and it helped give him something of infinite
value: British citizenship.

00:13:05.550 --> 00:13:07.010
The timing couldn't have been better.

00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:11.709
For years, Hitler had been working on reuniting
Germany and Austria; there was a failed coup,

00:13:11.709 --> 00:13:15.830
a treaty with Italy that promised to protect
Austria from German aggression, and even attempts

00:13:15.830 --> 00:13:19.200
by Mussolini's Italian troops to interfere
with the annexation.

00:13:19.200 --> 00:13:23.350
It happened anyway, and in 1938 Austria was
absorbed into the Third Reich.

00:13:23.350 --> 00:13:26.310
That had a devastating impact on the Wittgenstein
family.

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:31.080
Remember, they had been long-practicing Christians
by this time, but what was done in practice

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:35.350
and what was written on paper were, in their
case, two different things.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:40.260
There was still a record of those three Jewish
grandparents, and conversion or no conversion,

00:13:40.260 --> 00:13:44.790
that meant they were still considered Jewish
under the Nuremberg laws dictating racial

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:45.790
purity.

00:13:45.790 --> 00:13:50.820
None of them were able to vote or do seemingly
petty actions like sit on a park bench.

00:13:50.820 --> 00:13:53.170
For all of the siblings, it was dangerous.

00:13:53.170 --> 00:13:57.410
Paul in particular — a musical celebrity
of 1930s Vienna — was deeply troubled.

00:13:57.410 --> 00:14:01.390
He was no longer allowed to work in the field
of the arts, and more troubling, he was in

00:14:01.390 --> 00:14:06.790
violation of another law: Section 2 of the
Nuremberg Law for Protection of German Blood

00:14:06.790 --> 00:14:07.980
and German Honor.

00:14:07.980 --> 00:14:12.420
He wasn't married, but he was the father of
two children — and their mother was German.

00:14:12.420 --> 00:14:17.050
Had they been a normal, everyday sort of family,
they may have been able to flee without attracting

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:18.830
the attention of the Third Reich.

00:14:18.830 --> 00:14:22.720
But they were one of the wealthiest in Europe,
and what followed was a complex bit of legal

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.300
and financial maneuvering that really didn't
work out well for anyone.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:30.620
In 1939, the two surviving Wittengenstein
brothers (brother Kurt had also committed

00:14:30.620 --> 00:14:35.230
suicide at the end of World War One) appealed
their case based on the fact that a single

00:14:35.230 --> 00:14:38.780
grandparent was the bastard child of a gentile.

00:14:38.780 --> 00:14:42.980
Along with the appeal went a significant bribe:
they handed over most of their fortune to

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:48.240
the Nazi regime but in turn, their sisters
Hermine and Helene were granted half-breed

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:52.480
status and were safe from the relentless march
of the Nazi war machine.

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:57.060
It's undeniable that the Wittgenstein fortune
aided the advancement of the Third Reich,

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:00.720
and if there ever was a philosophical question
that needed debate, it's that one.

00:15:00.720 --> 00:15:04.640
What was the true price paid for the safety
of the Wittgenstein sisters?

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:05.640
Was it worth it?

00:15:05.640 --> 00:15:07.870
Should they have paid, and would you have?

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:11.970
It's a question that can only be answered
by those faced with the impossible question.

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:15.800
It was Paul who was the driving force behind
the decision to transfer much of the family's

00:15:15.800 --> 00:15:20.640
fortune from a trust in a Swiss bank account
to the coffers of the Third Reich; he, the

00:15:20.640 --> 00:15:24.890
oldest sibling, would be the last to die,
and after he made the decision to save them,

00:15:24.890 --> 00:15:27.230
he would never see any of his siblings again.

00:15:27.230 --> 00:15:31.811
For his part during the war, Ludwig Wittgenstein
worked as a porter at Guy's Hospital in London…

00:15:31.811 --> 00:15:36.190
and here is where we touch on a few of the
things that historians and scholars have never

00:15:36.190 --> 00:15:38.810
truly been able to answer.

00:15:38.810 --> 00:15:44.590
The mysteries of Wittgenstein

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:47.890
Wittgenstein's writings make it clear that
there are some unresolved issues simmering

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:52.540
away in the background, and it's a fascinating
glimpse into the mind of a man so obsessed

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:53.540
with logic.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:57.100
For one, it's never quite clear whether or
not he considered himself Jewish.

00:15:57.100 --> 00:16:01.220
Occasionally, he did call himself a "Jewish
thinker," while at other times, he condemned

00:16:01.220 --> 00:16:05.010
what he believed was a misrepresentation of
his ancestry.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:08.890
Similarly, Wittgenstein found himself fighting
an almost constant battle with his sexuality.

00:16:08.890 --> 00:16:13.310
At the time, it wasn't just taboo to be gay,
it was illegal and would have been met with

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:14.670
outrage and ostracization at the very least.

00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:19.490
It's impossible to tell what kind of effect
bearing that sort of burden had on his writing

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:24.269
and his philosophy, but it's also been suggested
that he gravitated toward teachings like Freud's,

00:16:24.269 --> 00:16:28.019
who suggested people were universally attracted
to both sexes.

00:16:28.019 --> 00:16:32.890
It was an idea that was safely mainstream,
and may have been a comforting bit of modern

00:16:32.890 --> 00:16:33.890
thought.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:38.100
There was another constant and underlying
theme throughout not just Wittgenstein's writings,

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:41.960
but through the diaries and journals of those
who knew him.

00:16:41.960 --> 00:16:46.800
He spoke often of his thoughts of suicide,
told those closest to him about his sense

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:51.230
of unrelenting shame and lonliness, and of
the possibility of becoming just another one

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.649
of his siblings to take that final step.

00:16:53.649 --> 00:16:58.040
His friend David Hume Pisent wrote this heartbreaking
glimpse into his psyche: "... he had suffered

00:16:58.040 --> 00:17:02.201
from terrific loneliness for the past nine
years, that he had thought of suicide then,

00:17:02.201 --> 00:17:06.100
and that he felt ashamed of never daring to
kill himself [...] he told me that all his

00:17:06.100 --> 00:17:10.169
life there had hardly been a day in which
he had not at one time or another thought

00:17:10.169 --> 00:17:12.689
of suicide as a possibility."

00:17:12.689 --> 00:17:15.779
Wittgenstein did ultimately die young, but
he did not take his own life.

00:17:15.779 --> 00:17:20.569
For two decades, he struggled to put together
another book but, always the perfectionist,

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:24.179
he consistently failed in putting together
something that would live up to the vision

00:17:24.179 --> 00:17:25.659
of what he believed it should be.

00:17:25.659 --> 00:17:30.149
His later works were only published posthumously,
and he didn't live to see the influence he

00:17:30.149 --> 00:17:32.460
had on 20th century philosophy.

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:35.710
In 1947, Wittgenstein resigned from his post
at Cambridge.

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:40.129
Much like he had once retreated to the quiet
solitude of the fjords of Norway, he later

00:17:40.129 --> 00:17:44.149
did the same and headed off to the remote
wilds of Connemara in Ireland.

00:17:44.149 --> 00:17:46.950
The peaceful solitude was not to last.

00:17:46.950 --> 00:17:51.769
In 1949, he was diagnosed with prostate cancer,
and by 1951, he knew he didn't have long to

00:17:51.769 --> 00:17:52.769
live.

00:17:52.769 --> 00:17:55.960
He moved into a home with his doctor, and
died on April 29, 1951.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:18:17.399
Reportedly, his last words were, "Tell them
I've had a wonderful life."

